{
  "requirement": "TBV owns the model conversation, supported backend integrations, tool\ndispatch, and structured submission used by its ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.tool_agent` is an umbrella requirement. It says TBV owns four things for its ask builder: the model conversation, the supported backend integrations, tool dispatch, and structured submission. Details such as isolation, submission acceptance and invalidity, the MCP description, fixed tools, provider failure, and per-backend config are in refining requirements. Those need no tests here. I reviewed the supplied tests and the tests I found through targeted search, and I found no gap.\n\n**Conversation.** These tests drive the tool-agent loop with scripted providers and assert what each turn sends and receives:\n- `agent/tests.rs#fn[application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation]` runs a four-turn conversation. It checks that every request carries all tools, that tool results are delivered in order in the next turn, and that paging and dependency observations are right.\n- `agent_executions_isolate_conversations_and_dependency_traces`, `concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads` and `behavioral_adequacy_uses_domain_contents_and_returns_a_typed_fingerprinted_answer` cover concurrent conversations. They also check the system and user messages, the typed `submit_result` schema, and the returned typed answer.\n\n**Tool dispatch.**\n- `application_tool_factories_create_fresh_state_for_each_agent_call` checks that application tools are dispatched and get fresh state per call. The counters reach 1 and 2 in each call and never 3.\n- `application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation` mixes graph tools and application tools in the same turn.\n- `source_tools_keep_search_discovery_separate_from_delivered_source_reads` dispatches `search_text` and `read_source`. It asserts that only the delivered read produces a dependency.\n\n**Structured submission.** `submit_result` is exercised in every scripted test. Its schema is asserted, including `required` and the `result` type. The result is returned typed, with fingerprints and dependencies stored and reloaded.\n\n**Supported backends.** `AgentConfig` has three backends: Bedrock, OpenAI and Ollama.\n- `configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport]` runs two independent calls over a local HTTP transport. It asserts the model, the output limit, the tool list and order, and the `submit_result` schema. It also asserts that the tool result is delivered back on the second turn and that no private config reaches the saved evidence.\n- `supplied_ollama_backend_delivers_tools_and_results_over_native_chat` does the same over a native `/api/chat` transport. It checks the path, the bearer key, the model, `stream`, options, the tools, and that the tool result is delivered on turn 2.\n- Bedrock has no end-to-end transport test. `BedrockConfig` has no endpoint override, so a full conversation would need live AWS services. The project sets none up, so the network exemption applies to that path.\n- The Bedrock-specific logic that has conditionals is tested directly in `providers.rs`:\n  - `bedrock_native_option_types_are_checked_and_only_max_tokens_is_defaulted`\n  - `cache_checkpoints_are_requested_for_caching_models`\n  - `typed_client_calls_preserve_order_and_native_history`\n\nThe tests carry `{test}` references to the subject, so there is no marking gap.",
  "references": "8ed00f348732b47fae1c4f4a3cd306ae69762b9f4bb6f22913ad71ae1d0c3b86",
